#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192390 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea184595-346e-5c0c-bb95-8779d73f6f6b" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Bernoulli Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "3ad1cf25-eb36-5946-9e06-16896691aaea" :question/math-academy-id "e-10112" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{10}$ is an I.I.D random sample where $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{10}$, you're given that there are $7$ successes. Find the log-likelihood function of the sample." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= \\ln L(θ) \\\\\n&= \\ln (∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=10$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta$. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta) = \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x=0, 1\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (θ^{x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{1 - x_{i}}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (θ^{x_{i}}) + \\ln ((1 - θ)^{1 - x_{i}}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}\\ln θ + (1 - x_{i})\\ln (1 - θ) \\\\\n&= \\ln θ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + \\ln (1 - θ)∑_(i = 1)^(n)(1 - x_{i}) \\\\\n&= S\\ln θ + (n - S)\\ln (1 - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nis the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $7$ successes in the sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= 7\\ln θ + (10 - 7)\\ln (1 - θ) \\\\\n&= 7\\ln θ + 3\\ln (1 - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192519 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50df0342-4c06-5c50-90ae-812403a69cee" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Binomial Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "4d597364-c8aa-5848-843b-bfc870ebe737" :question/math-academy-id "e-11119" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1, X_2, X_3$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim B(5,\\theta)$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta$. You're given the particular sample\n$x_1 = 4, \\qquad x_2 = 1, \\qquad x_3 = 1$.\nFind the log-likelihood function of this sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n$f(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{5}{x} \\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{5-x}, \\qquad x = 0,1, \\ldots, 5$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= \\ln L(θ) \\\\\n&= \\ln (∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i}, θ)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (f(x_{i}, θ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n = 3$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N = 5$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, θ) &= (\\frac{N}{x})θ^{x}(1 - θ)^{N - x}, x = 0, 1, …, N \\\\\n&= (\\frac{5}{x})θ^{x}(1 - θ)^{5 - x}, x = 0, 1, …, 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln ((\\frac{5}{x_{i}})θ^{x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{5 - x_{i}}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(\\ln (\\frac{5}{x_{i}}) + \\ln (θ^{x_{i}}) + \\ln ((1 - θ)^{5 - x_{i}})) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (\\frac{5}{x_{i}}) + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}\\ln θ + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(5 - x_{i})\\ln (1 - θ) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (\\frac{5}{x_{i}}) + \\ln θ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + \\ln (1 - θ)∑_(i = 1)^(n)(5 - x_{i}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (\\frac{5}{x_{i}}) + S\\ln θ + (5n - S)\\ln (1 - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (\\frac{5}{x_{i}}) &= \\ln (\\frac{5}{x_{1}}) + \\ln (\\frac{5}{x_{2}}) + \\ln (\\frac{5}{x_{3}}) \\\\\n&= \\ln (∏_(i = 1)^(n)(\\frac{5}{x_{i}}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta)$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = \\ln\\left(\\prod_{i=1}^n {5 \\choose x_i}\\right) + S\\ln \\theta + (5n - S) \\ln(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∏_(i = 1)^(n)(\\frac{5}{x_{i}}) &= (\\frac{5}{4}) \\cdot (\\frac{5}{1}) \\cdot (\\frac{5}{1}) \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS = 4 + 1 + 1 = 6\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= \\ln (125) + 6\\ln θ + (5 \\cdot 3 - 6)\\ln (1 - θ) \\\\\n&= \\ln 125 + 6\\ln θ + 9\\ln (1 - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0ab99b4-70b8-5638-bbad-763ef925021c" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Poisson Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "5df5188f-3dfe-599f-b6f3-16b93c433ab1" :question/math-academy-id "e-11120" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,X_3$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\theta)$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta$. You're given the particular sample\n$x_1 = 3,\\qquad x_2=4,\\qquad x_3 = 1$.\n\nFind the log-likelihood function of the sample." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= \\ln L(θ) \\\\\n&= \\ln (∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i}, θ)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (f(x_{i}, θ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta$. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, θ) &= e^{- θ} \\cdot \\frac{θ^{x}}{x!}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (e^{- θ} \\cdot \\frac{θ^{x_{i}}}{x_{i}!}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (e^{- θ}) + \\ln (θ^{x_{i}}) - \\ln (x_{i}!) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(- θ) + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (θ^{x_{i}}) - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (x_{i}!) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(- θ) + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}\\ln (θ) - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (x_{i}!) \\\\\n&= - θ∑_(i = 1)^(n)1 + \\ln θ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (x_{i}!) \\\\\n&=-nθ + \\ln θ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} - \\ln (∏_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}!)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∏_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}! &= 3! \\cdot 4! \\cdot 1! = 6 \\cdot 24 \\cdot 1 = 144, \\\\\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} &= 3 + 4 + 1 = 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= -3θ + 8\\ln θ - \\ln 144\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
